 Use `/whoami` to see the active scope, your tier (admin / user / unrestricted), and which slash commands you can run.
 
+## Fleet Pairing Owner Binding
+
+Requesting a Fleet pairing invitation from Telegram uses a separate, opt-in owner binding. It is
+**not** implied by `allow_from`, `TELEGRAM_ALLOWED_USERS`, the configured home channel, display names,
+or `allow_admin_from`. The latter grants slash-command administrator access and is not a Fleet
+pairing permission.
+
+To bind an explicit Telegram account to Fleet-pairing requests for this profile, configure its
+numeric Telegram user ID:
+
+```yaml
+platforms:
+  telegram:
+    fleet_pairing_owner_user_ids:
+      - "123456789"
+```
+
+This binding applies only to a current, gateway-verified private DM whose sender user ID matches the
+chat ID and whose sender also passes ordinary gateway access control. Group chats, missing or
+conflicting identity, and an omitted/empty owner list fail closed. It grants no slash commands,
+general chat access, or Dashboard session.
+
+The HTTP invitation API still requires an authenticated non-device Dashboard owner session over
+HTTPS. The host-side `python -m hermes_cli.fleet_pairing_cli invite` command is a separate local owner
+path: it acts directly on the current Hermes home and does not require an interactive Dashboard
+cookie. For a verified, explicitly bound private-DM request, an agent may use that host command and
+return the link only in that same private DM. A context-check message is not an invitation request.
+
+Pairing invitation URLs are bearer credentials. Never put them in groups, diagnostic reports,
+attachments, or logs; outside the explicitly requested owner-bound private-DM flow, use the secure
+local QR workflow.
+
